
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto, it controls how Hibernate handles your database schema when
your Spring Boot application starts.

spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update

What does ddl-auto mean?

DDL = Data Definition Language

Things like:
CREATE TABLE
ALTER TABLE
DROP TABLE

Hibernate can automatically perform these schema operations based on your @Entity classes.

Main options

Value	               What it does
none	               Hibernate does nothing to the schema
validate	           Checks whether DB schema matches entities; doesn't change DB
update	               Tries to update schema to match entities
create	               Creates schema when application starts; existing schema may be dropped
create-drop	           Creates schema at startup and drops it when application stops

Example

@Entity
public class Employee {

    @Id
    private Long id;

    private String name;
}

With:

spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update

Hibernate can create/update a table similar to:

CREATE TABLE employee (
    id BIGINT NOT NULL,
    name VARCHAR(255),
    PRIMARY KEY (id)
);

If later you add:

private String email;

Hibernate with update may alter the table to add the column.

validate vs update

This is especially important for interviews:

spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate
means:
"Check that my database schema matches my entities, but don't modify anything."

Whereas:
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
means:
"Try to modify the schema so that it matches my entities."

For local development:is commonly convenient.

For production, blindly relying on update is generally avoided. Teams typically use database
migration tools such as Flyway or Liquibase and often configure Hibernate with:

spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate

Interview answer:

"ddl-auto is a Hibernate configuration that controls automatic database schema generation
and validation based on JPA entity mappings."